
1:git init->The git init command is used to create a new,
    empty Git repository in the current or a specified directory.

Creates a hidden .git subdirectory: This folder is the core of your repository.
It contains all the necessary metadata, objects, references, and
configuration files that Git uses to track changes, manage history,
and handle branches.

Initializes the repository structure: Inside the .git folder,
it sets up basic subdirectories (like objects, refs/heads, refs/tags, and hooks)
and files (like HEAD and config).

Sets up an initial branch: It configures a default branch, often named main or master.
Does not track files automatically: The command only creates the potential for
version control;
it doesn't start tracking your existing project files right away.
You must use git add followed by git commit to start recording revisions

2:git add.->Run the git add . command to mark the files to be version controlled.
The names of the files to be added to the Git repository come after the git add command.
The . means adding all files in the current directory.

3:git status ->to preview the commit.

4:git commit -m "First commit."->command to commit the marked files into the repository.
This step saves the changes as a snapshot.


what is the difference between git add and commit:

git add and git commit are two separate,
sequential steps in the Git workflow that work together to save changes.

->git add is used to add modified or new files from your working directory
to the staging area (also known as the "index").
This is an intermediate area that lets you select exactly which changes
you want to include in the next commit.

->git commit takes everything that is currently in the staging area and
permanently saves a snapshot of that content
to your local repository's history. This snapshot is assigned a unique identifier
(a SHA-1 hash) and is a point you
can revert back to later if needed.

Summary of Differences
Feature 	git add	git commit
Purpose	Stages changes for the next commit.
    Records staged changes as a permanent snapshot in the local repository.
Location affected
    Moves changes from the working directory to the staging area.
    Moves changes from the staging area to the local repository.
Permanence	Changes are not yet a permanent part of the project history.
    Changes become a part of the project's permanent history (locally).
Workflow Order	Must be used before git commit for new or modified files.
    Occurs after git add to finalize the changes into a checkpoint.


git add vs git commit vs git push
The git add command moves changes from your working directory (the place where you edit files)into this staging area
(an index, or record of what’s about to be committed).
example:git add app.js


Then git commit converts whatever is staged into a new snapshot.
When you look at your repository’s commit history later using git log,
you’ll see these messages along with commit hashes and authorship details.

Finally, git push transfers those snapshots from your local repository to a
remote repository, making them visible to your team.

How git push shares changes with others
Up until now, all changes you’ve made have been local.
Even if you create multiple commits, those commits live only on your machine,
inside your local Git repository. To share these changes with others, you use git push.
git push sends your local commits to a remote repository
(often hosted on platforms like GitHub, GitLab, or Bitbucket).
 A typical command is: git push origin main


Here, origin is the default name for the remote repository you cloned from, and
main is the branch you’re pushing to.
When you run this command, Git compares your local branch’s commits to the
remote’s commits and
uploads any commits that are missing on the remote side.

Under the hood, git push communicates with the remote repository through
the Git protocol, SSH, or HTTPS.
 It transfers your commits, along with any objects
(such as file changes and directory snapshots) that the remote repository
doesn’t have yet.
 Once complete, your new commits appear on the remote repository’s history.
 This means your teammates can now git pull those changes into their own local
 environments and continue from where you left off.


git status :The git status command is a fundamental Git tool that displays the
current state of your working directory and staging area.
 It helps you monitor changes and decide which files should be part of the next commit.

 Scope: Inspects the working directory and the staging area (index).
Purpose: Shows which files have been:
Modified but not yet staged.
Staged (added to the staging area) and are ready for the next commit.
Untracked (new files that Git is not yet monitoring).
Deleted.

Output: The output is a real-time "health check" that often includes helpful
hints on what commands to use next, like git add or git restore.
History: It provides no information about the project's commit history.


git log:
git log is used for reviewing the project's history and answers the question,
"What changes have already been saved to the repository?"

Scope: Operates only on the committed history of the repository.
Purpose: Displays committed snapshots, allowing you to list, filter,
and search for specific past changes.
Output: Each entry typically includes:
A unique commit hash (SHA-1 checksum).
The author's name and email.
The date and time of the commit.
The commit message.


Question 1: what is the difference between git status and git log
 The core difference is that git status shows the current state of your local
 working directory and staging area,
 while git log shows the history of committed changes


Question 2:The main difference is that git push safely adds local commits
            to the remote repository,
 while git push --force overwrites the remote branch's history with your local history,
 potentially causing data loss for other team members

