git pull(git fetch + git merge )->
The primary command for updating your local repository with changes from
    a remote repository is git pull.
What git pull Does
git pull is a shortcut command that performs two operations in one step:
git fetch: This downloads new commits and data from the remote repository
            to your local Git repository but does not apply them to your working files.
git merge: This automatically integrates the fetched changes into your
            current working branch and working directory files.


git rebase
git rebase moves or rewrites commits so that your branch appears as if
    it was created from a new base.
It gives you a clean, linear commit history.

🔵 1. Why Rebase? (Visual Explanation)

Suppose your repo looks like this:
A -- B -- C   (main)
       \
        D -- E (feature/login)
While you worked on commits D and E, new commit C appeared on main.

❓ What you want:

You want your feature to sit on top of the updated main.

🔵 2. Rebase vs Merge (Visual)
Option 1: git merge main

Keeps history but adds a merge commit.
A -- B -- C -------- M      (feature)
       \           /
        D -- E ---        (merge commit M)
History becomes branched and sometimes messy.

Option 2: git rebase main

Rewrites your feature onto the updated main:
A -- B -- C -- D' -- E'   (feature)
Cleaner. Looks like you developed the feature after commit C.

Meaning:

feature/login was created from commit B

main has moved ahead to commit C

Your branch has commits D and E

You want to rebase feature/login onto main so that D and E sit after C.

⭐ Goal After Rebase
A -- B -- C -- D' -- E'   (feature/login)
(D and E become new commits D' and E' rewritten on top of C.)

🧭 Step-by-Step Git Commands
1️⃣ Checkout your feature branch

You must be on the branch you want to rebase.
git checkout feature/login

2️⃣ Rebase onto main

This moves your branch commits (D, E) onto the latest main (C):
git rebase main
What Git does internally:

Finds the fork point (commit B)

Takes commits D and E aside

Resets your branch to look like main (ending at C)

Replays D and E onto C as NEW commits (D', E')

working directory(git add)->staging directory(git commit)->
                                         local repository(git push)->remote directory.

pull(fetch+merge)->
fetch(download from remote to local directory but did not merge to working directory)
        +merge(integrate the changes to the working directory).


merge ->suppose there is new commit on the feature and main branch comes at the
            same so merge will merge both of them by preserve the history.

while rebase -> in rebase feature commits will append after the latest main by
        deleting the history (making cleaner history).